### 🟢 Data Market purchase authorization does not bind `buyer` address to the signing key, so any party can debit another account

**Severity:** LOW · **CWE-347**

`PurchaseAuthorizationV5::verify_pq` verifies the ML-DSA-87 signature against a caller-supplied public key (`buyer_ml_dsa_87_pk`) but never checks that this key derives the `buyer` address also carried in the same struct. The address is only covered as plain bytes inside the signed digest, so an attacker can generate their own key pair, sign a purchase authorization naming any victim address as `buyer`, and have `settle_purchase_with_grant` debit that victim's balance to a listing they control. The signature check passes because the attacker signed with the key they supplied.

```suggestion
    pub fn verify_pq(&self, expected_chain_id: u64) -> Result<(), DataMarketError> {
        if self.chain_id != expected_chain_id {
            return Err(DataMarketError::ChainIdMismatch {
                signed: self.chain_id,
                expected: expected_chain_id,
            });
        }
        validate_public_key(SchemeId::ML_DSA_87, &self.buyer_ml_dsa_87_pk)
            .map_err(|_| DataMarketError::InvalidBuyerSignature)?;
        // The address is not a signature input: it is what the debited account
        // is looked up by. Verifying the signature against a key supplied in the
        // same struct proves only that whoever built this struct holds *a* key.
        // It must be the key of `buyer`.
        let derived = Address::from_ml_dsa_87_public_key(&self.buyer_ml_dsa_87_pk)
            .map_err(|_| DataMarketError::InvalidBuyerSignature)?;
        if derived != self.buyer {
            return Err(DataMarketError::InvalidBuyerSignature);
        }
        let digest = self.signing_digest();
        verify(
            SchemeId::ML_DSA_87,
            &self.buyer_ml_dsa_87_pk,
            &digest,
            STORAGE_V2,
            &self.buyer_signature,
        )
        .map_err(|_| DataMarketError::InvalidBuyerSignature)
    }
```

