### 🟡 Garden leadership check trusts a round the proposer names, so any validator can propose out of turn

**Severity:** MEDIUM · Deep review

**TL;DR:** `GardenState::verify_block` accepts a block whose proposer equals `leader(header.round)`, and the proposer chooses that round, so any member leads a round it was never scheduled for and the other validators sign it.

**Impact:** Any Garden validator can produce the next block out of turn; when that block reaches the host first its transaction set and ordering are finalised instead of the scheduled leader's, so one member can take other members' block-production slots and bias what is confirmed.

**Root cause:**
- The leadership rule trusts a value the proposer writes into its own header: `leader(round)` is cyclic in `round`, so `self.leader(header.round) == header.proposer` in `verify_block` is satisfied by whichever proposer picks the matching round.
- Nothing else bounds the round: `verify_block` compares it against no reference, and `Node::consider` lets every non-host validator sign any proposal that verifies, while only the host refuses a round more than one round ahead of its own clock.
- So the rotation rule ('a leader that stays silent loses the slot') is enforced nowhere on the voting side, and a member proposes at `height + 1` in the round named after itself instead of waiting for its turn.

